2N,\nL51@P1: where C∗(Q/Z)= C(Zˆ) is generated by abstract roots of unity e(r), r ∈ Q/Z, and the elements n\nL52@P1: in the multiplicative semigroup are realized by isometries μn. The semigroup N acts on C∗(Q/Z) by\nL53@P1: endomorphisms\nL54@P1: ρn(e(r)) = μne(r)μ∗\nL55@P1: n = 1\nL56@P1: n\nL57@P1: \u0002\nL58@P1: s:ns=r\nL59@P1: e(s).\nL60@P1: The time evolution of the system is given by σt(e(r)) = e(r) and σt(μn)= nit μn, with generator the\nL61@P1: Hamiltonian H\u0004k = log(k)\u0004k, in a representation on \u00052(N). The partition function is the Riemann zeta\nL62@P1: function,\nL63@P1: Z(β) = Tr(e−βH )= \u0002\nL64@P1: k≥1\nL65@P1: k−β.\nL66@P1: The extremal low temperature KMS states are polylogarithm functions evaluated at roots of unity,\nL67@P1: normalized by the Riemann zeta function, while at zero temperature the KMS states and the symmetry\nL68@P1: action are related to cyclotomic fields with the Galois action of Gal(Qab/Q). We refer the reader to [2]\nL69@P1: and Chapter 3 of [8] for a detailed discussion of these properties.\nL70@P1: The Bost-Connes system was reinterpreted in [6] as a particular example of an “endomotive”,a\nL71@P1: projective system of zero-dimensional varieties with semigroup actions. The analytical properties of\nL72@P1: the quantum statistical mechanical system and its relation to L-functions were also generalized to this\nL73@P1: context. Moreover, in [7], the Bost-Connes endomotive was related to the geometry of the “field with one\nL74@P1: element\" F1 and its system of extensions (given by roots of unity) as defined by Kapranov-Smirnov [11].\nL75@P1: In [5], the integral Bost-Connes system considered in [7] was related to Witt rings and the Frobenius\nL76@P1: and Verschiebung operators. In [18], generalizations and categorifications of the Bost-Connes system\nL77@P1: were constructed, which include Weil numbers and motives over finite fields.\nL78@P1: Further aspects of the relation between the Bost-Connes system and F1-geometry were considered\nL79@P1: in [15] and [17]. Manin proposed in [15] to use the Habiro ring of q-functions, [9], as a good notion of\nL80@P1: analytic geometry and analytic functions over F1, and analogs of the Bost-Connes system based on the\nL81@P1: Habiro ring were constructed in [17]. Relations between the Habiro ring and motives were discussed in\nL82@P1: [14].\nL83@P1: 1.2. The Integral Bost-Connes Algebra\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://alainconnes.org/wp-content/uploads/selecta.ps-2.pdf)\nciteturn141view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 89; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn121view4\",\"pattern\":\"equivalent\"}); Total lines: 3978\nL17@P0: VIII The trace formula in the global case, and elimination of δ.\nL18@P0: Appendix I, Proof of theorem 1.\nL19@P0: Appendix II, Explicit formulas.\nL20@P0: Appendix III, Distribution trace formulas.\nL21@P0-1: 1Introduction\nL22@P1: We shall give in this paper a spectral interpretation of the zeros of the Rie\u0002mann zeta function and a geometric framework in which one can transpose\nL23@P1: the ideas of algebraic geometry involving the action of the Frobenius and the\nL24@P1: Lefschetz formula. The spectral interpretation of the zeros of zeta will be as\nL25@P1: an absorption spectrum, i.e., as missing spectral lines. All zeros will play a\nL26@P1: role in the spectral side of the trace formula, but while the critical zeros will\nL27@P1: appear perse, the noncritical ones will appear as resonances and enter in the\nL28@P1: trace formula through their harmonic potential with respect to the critical\nL29@P1: line. Thus the spectral side is entirely canonical, and by proving positivity of\nL30@P1: the Weil distribution, we shall show that its equality with the geometric side,\nL31@P1: i.e., the global trace formula, is equivalent to the Riemann Hypothesis for all\nL32@P1: L-functions with Gr¨ossencharakter.\nL33@P1: We shall model our discussion on the Selberg trace formula, but it differs\nL34@P1: from the latter in several important respects. We shall first explain in partic\u0002ular why a crucial negative sign in the analysis of the statistical fluctuations\nL35@P1: of the zeros of zeta indicates that the spectral interpretation should be as an\nL36@P1: absorption spectrum, or equivalently should be of a cohomological nature. As\nL37@P1: it turns out, the geometric framework involves an innocent looking space, the\nL38@P1: space X of Adele classes, where two adeles which belong to the same orbit\nL39@P1: of the action of GL1(k)(k a global field), are considered equivalent. The\nL40@P1: group Ck = GL1(A)/GL1(k) of Idele classes (which is the class field theory\nL41@P1: counterpart of the Galois group) acts by multiplication on X.\nL42@P1: Our first preliminary result (Theorem 1 of Section III) gives a spectral\nL43@P1: interpretation of the critical zeros of zeta and L functions on a global field\nL44@P1: k from the action of the Idele class group on a space of square integrable\nL45@P1: functions on the space X = A/k∗ of Adele classes. Corollary 2 gives the cor\u0002responding computation of the spectral trace. This result is only preliminary\nL46@P1: because it requires the use of an unnatural parameter δ which plays the role\nL47@P1: of a Sobolev exponent and allows us to see the absorption spectrum as a point\nL48@P1: spectrum.\nL49@P1: Our second preliminary result is a formal computation (Section VI) of the\nL50@P1: character of the representation of the Idele class group on the above L2 space.\nL51@P1: This formal computation gives the Weil distribution which is the essential\nL52@P1: ingredient of the Riemann-Weil explicit formula. At this point (which was\nL53@P1: the situation in [Co]), the main problems are to give a rigorous meaning to\nL54@P1: the formal trace computation and to eliminate the unwanted parameter δ.\nL55@P1-2: 2These two problems will be solved in the present paper. We first prove\nL56@P2: a trace formula (Theorem 3 of Section V) for the action of the multiplicative\nL57@P2: group K∗ of a local field K on the Hilbert space L2(K), and (Theorem 4 of\nL58@P2: Section VII) a trace formula for the action of the multiplicative group CS of\nL59@P2: Idele classes associated to a finite set S of places of a global field k, on the\nL60@P2: Hilbert space of square integrable functions L2(XS), where XS is the quotient\nL61@P2: of \u0001\nL62@P2: v∈S kv by the action of the group O∗\nL63@P2: S of Sc-units of k. In both cases we\nL64@P2: obtain exactly the terms of the Weil explicit formulas which belong to the\nL65@P2: finite set of places. This result is quite important since the space XS is highly\nL66@P2: nontrivial as soon as the cardinality of S is larger or equal to 3. Indeed this\nL67@P2: quotient space is nontype I in the sense of Noncommutative Geometry, and it\nL68@P2: is reassuring that the trace formula continues to hold there.\nL69@P2: We check in detail (Theorem 6 of Appendix II) that the rewriting of the\nL70@P2: Weil explicit formulas which is predicted by the global trace formula is correct.\nL71@P2: Finally, we eliminate in Section VIII using ideas that are common both to\nL72@P2: the Selberg trace formula and to the standard explanation of the absorption\nL73@P2: lines in physics, the unpleasant parameter δ which appeared as a label of the\nL74@P2: function spaces of Section III. We write the global trace formula as an analogue\nL75@P2: of the Selberg trace formula. The validity of the trace formula for any finite\nL76@P2: set of places follows from Theorem 4 of Section VII, but in the global case\nL77@P2: is left open and shown (Theorem 5 of Section VIII) to be equivalent to the\nL78@P2: validity of the Riemann Hypothesis for all L functions with Gr¨ossencharakter.\nL79@P2: This equivalence, together with the plausibility of a direct proof of the trace\nL80@P2: formula along the lines of Theorem 